* text=auto
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.psd1 text eol=crlf

# GENERATED FILES: LF EVERYWHERE, NOT NEGOTIABLE.
#
# scripts/Generate-Policy.ps1 renders these and CI check `generated-match-config` compares them
# to the config BYTE FOR BYTE. The generator always emits LF - that determinism is the whole
# reason a byte comparison counts as evidence.
#
# Under the `* text=auto` above these are checked out with the platform's native ending, so a
# Windows clone gets CRLF while the generator still renders LF: the check fails on a tree nobody
# has touched, and passes on the ubuntu runner, which is the worst way for it to be wrong. It
# passed here only because the working copy had never been re-checked-out since being written.
docs/POLICY.md text eol=lf
.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md text eol=lf

# FROZEN FIXTURES. Inspector output committed under corpus/ is cited by blob sha, so its bytes
# must be the same on every clone. The inspector writes LF.
corpus/*.jsonl text eol=lf

# THE FORENSIC CHAIN. LF, for the same reason the generated files above are LF, but with a
# sharper failure: `self` is a sha256 over the record, and the file is the only copy of the
# history it attests to.
#
# Measured on a cold clone of f1aeb60 on Windows: this file checks out at 534 bytes with one
# CRLF pair, against 533 bytes and none in a worktree that had never been re-checked-out. The
# parser trims, so `-Verify` returned OK on both - the conversion is SILENT, which is worse
# than a loud break. It means the bytes on one clone are not the bytes on another while both
# report the same tip, and an anchor is only worth what the bytes under it are.
#
# `-Append` writes LF. Under `* text=auto` a Windows checkout was free to hand it back CRLF.
.continuity/forensic.jsonl text eol=lf
